Current original Zero workflow (0.12)

Use the native tools on Linux or their .exe versions on Windows:
  zero-integrate PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-build PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-check PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_TEST_DIRECTORY
All destination paths must be new. The integrator preserves authored sources;
the builder checks its compiler fixed point and builds the original targets;
the verifier checks language behavior and host file operations in process.
See tools/integrate.ztext, tools/build.ztext and checks/README.ztext.
Read these documents with zero-text read FILE.

The notes below preserve earlier implementation and test history. Any old
shell/Python runner names, generated artifacts, JSON reports or commands are
historical references: they were removed from the active project and are not
current runnable instructions or current-release validation. Native host
checks do not replace BIOS/UEFI, persistence or physical-hardware testing.

Historical implementation notes

448 KiB boot/container capacity verification

Run from the project directory:
python3 review-tests/boot-capacity/run.py

The runner uses the private capacity candidate at
build/network-ports/capacity-stage2 when present, otherwise build/zero.
That must be the own Zero compiler with the 458752-byte output limit.
No assembler, linker or borrowed guest implementation is used.

Five actual Zero sources are compiled: BIOS, AMD64/IA32 EFI loaders and the
FAT/ISO writers. The original Zero slot-tail probe is compiled separately for
both kernels. Its anchor offset is measured and substituted before the final
compile, then verified stable. Test bytes are placed beyond executable code,
including both sides of the previous384KiB boundary and byte458751.

The writers build a private16MiB FAT image and17,776,640-byte optical ISO.
Python independently walks the FAT chains and ISO records, checking all
payloads and endian fields. Short/excess/invalid inputs and read/write failures
must produce documented errors. Four QEMU pc/TCG boots execute the whole-slot
probe in BIOS32/64 and IA32/AMD64 OVMF. QEMU, firmware and Python are external
test infrastructure. No writable data disk is attached to these VMs.

Internal generated binaries, firmware variable copies and images are under a
fresh build/boot-capacity.* directory; those are not distribution artifacts.
Evidence is under verification/boot-capacity-448k. The final integrated OS
uses a different kernel/ISO and requires its own VM results.

The runner contains the current workspace paths for QEMU/OVMF; adjust those
host-only paths when reproducing on another machine. It never runs build.sh
or replaces the project's current ISO or user disks.
